    "body": "Research for slice 3 (the laser post), from the public Grbl wiki page \"Grbl v1.1 Laser Mode\" (https://github.com/gnea/grbl/wiki/Grbl-v1.1-Laser-Mode):\n\n- `$32=1` turns laser mode on. Then Grbl moves through consecutive moves with a new `S` value without a stop. Each `M3` / `M4` / `M5` state change still stops the motion.\n- A `G0` move never turns the laser on and always turns it off (\"strictly a safety measure\").\n- `M3` is constant power: the laser keeps its power while the machine moves, accelerates or stops. `M4` is dynamic power: the power follows the speed, and the laser is off when the machine does not move.\n- Thus a pierce dwell at one point gives no power under `M4`. A stationary pierce needs `M3` (this is our inference; the page does not state it in these words).\n- `S0` turns the laser off without a change of the spindle state.\n- The page does not state the `S` scale. The Grbl configuration page states that `$30` is the `S` value of the full PWM output. So the post must know `$30` to write a percent as `S`.\n\nPlan for slice 3 (recommended, recorded in the decision file of that PR):\n1. A Grbl laser post that writes `M4` for the cut moves and `M3` with `G4 P<s>` for the pierce dwell. Each contour ends with `S0` or `M5`, and the program ends with `M5`.\n2. The power percent goes to `S` against a stated `$30` value. The default is 1000 (the Grbl default). The post writes the value it assumes in the header comment.\n3. The post refuses a program when the machine is not a laser cutter, and it never writes `M6` or coolant words.\n4. The header comment tells the operator to set `$32=1` before the run and `$32=0` before a return to milling.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
